1️⃣ Check if ANY duplicate exists (yes / no)
Pattern -> Seen-Before
Data structure -> HashSet
Code ->
int[] arr = {1, 2, 3, 4, 2};
Set<Integer> seen = new HashSet<>();
boolean hasDuplicate = false;
for (int x : arr) {
    if (!seen.add(x)) {
        hasDuplicate = true;
        break;
    }
}
System.out.println(hasDuplicate);

Why this works
set.add(x) returns false if already present
No order, no count → simplest structure


2️⃣ Find the FIRST repeating element
Pattern -> Seen-Before + Order
Data structure -> LinkedHashSet
Code ->
int[] arr = {4, 5, 1, 2, 5, 3};
Set<Integer> seen = new LinkedHashSet<>();
for (int x : arr) {
    if (!seen.add(x)) {
        System.out.println("First repeating: " + x);
        break;
    }
}

Why this works
Iteration order = insertion order
First element that fails add() is the answer

3️⃣ Find the FIRST NON-repeating character in a string
Pattern -> Frequency + Order
Data structure -> LinkedHashMap
Code ->
String s = "aabbcdd";
Map<Character, Integer> freq = new LinkedHashMap<>();
for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
    freq.put(c, freq.getOrDefault(c, 0) + 1);
}
for (Map.Entry<Character, Integer> e : freq.entrySet()) {
    if (e.getValue() == 1) {
        System.out.println("First non-repeating: " + e.getKey());
        break;
    }
}

Why this works
LinkedHashMap preserves insertion order
Frequency tells non-repeating
First key with count 1 wins

4️⃣ Longest substring without repeating characters (LC-3)
Pattern -> Variable Sliding Window + Seen-Before
Data structure -> HashMap<Character, Integer> (last index)
Code
String s = "abcabcbb";
Map<Character, Integer> lastIndex = new HashMap<>();
int left = 0;
int maxLen = 0;
int start = 0;
for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {
    char ch = s.charAt(right);//a
    if (lastIndex.containsKey(ch) && lastIndex.get(ch) >= left) {
        left = lastIndex.get(ch) + 1;
    }
    lastIndex.put(ch, right);

    if (right - left + 1 > maxLen) {
        maxLen = right - left + 1;
        start = left;
    }
}
System.out.println("Length: " + maxLen);
System.out.println("Substring: " + s.substring(start, start + maxLen));

Why this works
Window grows with right
Jumps left past last duplicate
Maintains uniqueness at all times

