SLIDING WINDOW PRACTICE SET (Variable Window)
🔑 Universal reminder (don’t skip)
Right always moves.
Left moves only when the rule is broken.
Answer updates only when the window is valid.

✅ Problem 1: Longest substring with at most 2 distinct characters
Example
Input: "eceba"
Output: 3   // "ece"

Step 1️⃣ Identify the rule
Window can have ≤ 2 distinct characters

Step 2️⃣ What breaks the rule?
Distinct character count > 2

Step 3️⃣ Data structure
HashMap<Character, Integer> → frequency

Step 4️⃣ Code
String s = "eceba";
Map<Character, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();
int left = 0, maxLen = 0;
for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {
    char ch = s.charAt(right);
    freq.put(ch, freq.getOrDefault(ch, 0) + 1);//e-1| c-1 |e-2 |b-1

    // rule broken
    while (freq.size() > 2) {1|2|2|3
        char leftChar = s.charAt(left);//e,c
        freq.put(leftChar, freq.get(leftChar) - 1);//e-1,c-0
        if (freq.get(leftChar) == 0) {
            freq.remove(leftChar);
        }
        left++;//1
    }

    // window valid
    maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, right - left + 1);// 0, 0-0+1=1 : 1| 1, 1-0+1=2 : 2| 2, 2-0+1=3 :3 |
}

System.out.println(maxLen);

🧠 Key takeaway
freq.size() controls validity
left moves only when size > 2

✅ Problem 2: Longest subarray with sum ≤ k
Example
arr = [2,1,5,1,3], k = 7
Output: 4   // [1,5,1]

Step 1️⃣ Rule
Window sum must be ≤ k

Step 2️⃣ What breaks the rule?
sum > k

Step 3️⃣ Data structure
Just an int sum

Step 4️⃣ Code
int[] arr = {2, 1, 5, 1, 3};
int k = 7;
int left = 0, sum = 0, maxLen = 0;
for (int right = 0; right < arr.length; right++) {
    sum += arr[right];

    // rule broken
    while (sum > k) {
        sum -= arr[left];
        left++;
    }

    // window valid
    maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, right - left + 1);
}
System.out.println(maxLen);

🧠 Key takeaway
Same structure as substring problems
Only the rule changes

✅ Problem 3: Longest substring with no repeating characters (revisited cleanly)
This is the one you found tricky — let’s make it mechanical.

Step 1️⃣ Rule
All characters must be unique

Step 2️⃣ What breaks the rule?
Character already exists inside window

Step 3️⃣ Data structure
HashMap<Character, Integer> → last index

Step 4️⃣ Code (clean & final)
String s = "abcabcbb";
Map<Character, Integer> last = new HashMap<>();
int left = 0, maxLen = 0;
for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {
    char ch = s.charAt(right);

    if (last.containsKey(ch) && last.get(ch) >= left) {
        left = last.get(ch) + 1;   // jump left
    }

    last.put(ch, right);
    maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, right - left + 1);
}
System.out.println(maxLen);

🧠 Why this version feels easier
No while loop
left jumps directly
Condition is explicit

