How do you decide between using while vs if when shrinking the window?

✅ Your idea (correct direction)
You said:
“when I have to loop condition till true then while. if once then if”
That’s right — but let’s translate it into sliding-window language, so your brain knows when to use which without guessing.

🔑 The REAL rule (lock this in)
Use while when the window may still be invalid after one shrink.
Use if when one adjustment always restores validity.

🧠 Why while is usually needed
Most sliding-window problems have rules like:
    distinct count ≤ K
    sum ≤ K
    no duplicates

When the rule breaks:
    Removing one element may NOT be enough
    You must keep shrinking until the rule is satisfied again

    Example: at most K distinct characters
    while (freq.size() > k) {
        // keep shrinking
    }

    Because:
    Removing one char may still leave freq.size() > k

👉 while is the default choice in sliding window

🧠 When if is enough (special case)

Use if only when:
You jump left directly to a known valid position
One move is guaranteed to fix the window

Example: Longest substring without repeating characters
if (lastIndex.get(ch) >= left) {
    left = lastIndex.get(ch) + 1;
}

Why if works here:
You jump past the duplicate in one step
Window becomes valid immediately

🔑 Interview-ready answer (use this)
“I use while when shrinking the window may require multiple steps to restore validity, and if when a single adjustment guarantees the window becomes valid.”

🧭 Final sliding-window cheat rule (remember this)
Situation	                Use
Need to shrink repeatedly	while
One jump fixes window	    if

========================================================================================================================
Why is while safer than if in most sliding-window problems?
while is safer because the window may remain invalid after one shrink, so we must keep shrinking until the condition is fully satisfied.

Why this matters
In most sliding-window problems, one removal isn’t guaranteed to fix the rule.
while ensures the window becomes valid before we update the answer.
Using if can leave the window partially invalid, leading to wrong results.

